1.What are directives, custom directives with explanation 
  Directives in Angular is a instruction to the dom, that will change the structure / behavior or appearence of the element.
  Angular mainly has two types of directives:  
  1. Structural Directives
  These modify the DOM structure by adding or removing elements.
  Examples include *ngIf, *ngFor, and *ngSwitch.  
  2. Attribute Directives
  These change the appearance or behavior of existing elements without changing the DOM structure.
  Examples include ngClass, ngStyle, and custom attribute directives.  
  Components are a special type of directive that include a template and styles, and Angular applications
  are primarily component-based.  
  Angular also allows creating custom structural and attribute directives to encapsulate reusable DOM behavior.

angular providing the predefined structural and attribute directives to handle most of the scenarions / real time uses.
but still we can also create custome directive 

EX: 2 type of code as per below statement: IMPORT
ElementRef is preferred for directives because it provides a stable reference to the host element and aligns 
with Angular’s component and directive lifecycle. $event.target is event-driven and may change depending on event 
bubbling, making it less predictable. Performance differences are negligible; correctness and maintainability 
matter more.
Attribute directive:
  @Directive({
    selector: '[appHighlight]'
  })
  export class HighlightDirective {
  
    constructor(private el: ElementRef) {}
  
    @HostListener('focus')
    onFocus() {
      this.el.nativeElement.style.background = 'yellow';
    }
  
    @HostListener('blur')
    onBlur() {
      this.el.nativeElement.style.background = '';
    }
  }
  
  working ex: 
  import { Directive, HostListener } from '@angular/core';
  
  @Directive({
    selector: '[appAttrColorDirective]',
    standalone: true
  })
  export class AttrColorDirectiveDirective {
  
    constructor() { }
  
    @HostListener('focus', ['$event'])
    onFocus(event: FocusEvent) {
      const target = event.target as HTMLElement | null;
      if (target) {
        target.style.backgroundColor = 'yellow';
      }
    }
  
  }  
  Usage:  
  <input appHighlight />    
  ✅ Reusable
  ✅ Clean templates
  ✅ No copy-paste JS

✅ Custom Structural Directive (example)
👉 Use case:
👉 Show element only if role is ADMIN
@Directive({
  selector: '[appHasRole]'
})
export class HasRoleDirective {
  constructor(
    private tpl: TemplateRef<any>,
    private vcr: ViewContainerRef
  ) {}

  @Input()
  set appHasRole(role: string) {
    if (role === 'ADMIN') {
      this.vcr.createEmbeddedView(this.tpl);
    } else {
      this.vcr.clear();
    }
  }
}
when custom directives 
TemplateRef decides WHAT to render, ViewContainerRef decides WHERE & WHEN to render.
1️⃣ What Angular sees in your template
<div *appHasRole="'ADMIN'">Admin Panel</div>
Angular NEVER keeps this as-is.
✅ Angular rewrites it internally to:
<ng-template [appHasRole]="'ADMIN'">
  <div>Admin Panel</div>
</ng-template>

🔴 Important facts:
<ng-template> is NOT rendered
It’s just a container for future rendering

2️⃣ TemplateRef — “WHAT can be rendered”
constructor(
  private tpl: TemplateRef<any>,
  private vcr: ViewContainerRef
) {}
🧩 TemplateRef (tpl)
Think of this as:
📄 Blueprint / HTML recipe
It contains:
<div>Admin Panel</div>
✅ Not in DOM
✅ Just stored in memory
✅ Cannot render itself

3️⃣ ViewContainerRef — “WHERE it goes”
🧩 ViewContainerRef (vcr)
Think of this as:
📍 Anchor point in the DOM
It knows:
Where to insert the view
Where to remove it from

4️⃣ The magic line 🚀
this.vcr.createEmbeddedView(this.tpl);
What happens step-by-step:
Angular takes TemplateRef
Creates a View instance (copy of template)
Inserts it into DOM at vcr location
✅ Element appears
✅ Change detection starts
✅ Child components initialize

5️⃣ Removing from DOM (clean & safe)
this.vcr.clear();
This does:
Removes DOM nodes
Destroys components
Executes lifecycle cleanup
✅ No memory leaks
✅ No dangling listeners
This is why Angular doesn’t want you to do:
element.remove()
============================================2. Decorator and type of decorator==================================================================
A decorator is a function that adds metadata to a class, method, property, or parameter without changing its actual code.
Angular uses decorators to store metadata, and later Angular reads that metadata at runtime to decide how to create, connect, and manage objects.




